          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"In throne visions, the living creatures are heavenly throne attendants identified with cherubim and often representative of animate creation; elsewhere, the phrase ordinarily denotes literal animate beings.\",\"book_summary\":\"A living creature symbolizes a nation or organized people, distinguished as the living counterpart of a beast or animal.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"A heavenly throne attendant in Ezekiel and Revelation, or generically an animate being outside those visions.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"A corporate political body: a nation or organized people.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book assigns a political-corporate referent to the sea creatures or living souls of Revelation 8:9 and 16:3. The consensus instead recognizes heavenly beings in throne visions and ordinary animate life in generic uses; it does not include nations as the phrase's established referent.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"For the cited Revelation occurrences, the book replaces living sea beings with organized human peoples. These cannot be the same immediate referent in the same symbolic reading without rejecting the ordinary animate-creature interpretation.\",\"rationale\":\"This is not merely a narrower definition of cherubim or animate life. It substitutes a nation or organized population for both consensus categories. Although Daniel 7 establishes that beasts can symbolize kings or kingdoms, the book's further equation of a living creature with an organized people is a distinct and incompatible identification for the cited Revelation texts.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Daniel 7:3,17 identifies beasts with kings but never identifies a “living creature” as a nation or explains the proposed beast/living-creature distinction. Revelation 8:9 and 16:3 describe living things dying in the sea, alongside ships, and contain no explicit indication that these creatures are organized peoples. The essential interpretive bridge is therefore unsupported by the printed citations.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
